{# attribute_extraction.j2 — system prompt for LLM attribute extraction
   Variables:
     attributes              : list[str] — attributes to pull out of descriptions
     prompt_options_string   : str       — pre-formatted "col: top values" hints
     example_desc            : str       — single illustrative description
     example_output          : str       — desired output for example_desc
     extraction_rules        : dict      — optional per-attribute normalization
                                           rules ({} for categories without them)
     cluster_descriptions    : bool      — whether inputs may carry sibling
                                           descriptions of the same product

   NOTE: access rule fields with BRACKETS (rule['values']), never rule.values —
   dicts expose .values as a method, which crashes Jinja.
#}
You are to pull out information from a product description.

Here are the attributes I am looking for:
{{ attributes }}
{% if cluster_descriptions | default(false) %}
Some inputs describe ONE physical product using SEVERAL descriptions written by
different branches. They are laid out like this:

  PRIMARY DESCRIPTION (this item): <the description belonging to this item>
  ADDITIONAL DESCRIPTIONS OF THE SAME PRODUCT (n):
    - <another branch's wording for the same product>
    - <...>
  CLUSTER ATTRIBUTE HINTS (verify against the descriptions): color=..., volume=...

How to use them:
- Treat every description as evidence about the SAME product, and return ONE set
  of attributes for it.
- Take the UNION of the attributes. Each description is usually missing
  something the others carry — if only one mentions the color, the material or
  the case pack, that value is still correct for the product. This is the whole
  reason you are given the extra descriptions.
- Prefer the more specific value. "12 oz paper hot cup" plus "12oz coated white
  paper hot cup" yields the coated, white reading.
- The PRIMARY DESCRIPTION wins on genuine conflict. If descriptions disagree on
  the same attribute in a way that cannot be reconciled (two different
  capacities, two different colors), use the primary item's value and ignore the
  others for that attribute alone.
- CLUSTER ATTRIBUTE HINTS come from an automated system and are NOT
  authoritative. Use one only when it is consistent with the descriptions, and
  override it when the descriptions contradict it. Where a hint lists several
  values separated by '/', the sources disagreed — pick the one the descriptions
  support, or leave the attribute blank.
- Never invent an attribute that appears in none of the descriptions. Leave it
  blank instead.
{% endif %}

Here is a look at the most common values for each of these attributes. This is not an exhaustive list, but it should help you understand what I am looking for.
You probably will need to pull out similar information for an attribute that's not on this list:

{{ prompt_options_string }}
{% if extraction_rules %}
Apply these category-specific normalization rules. When {% if cluster_descriptions | default(false) %}any of the descriptions{% else %}the description{% endif %} contains any of the listed variants/abbreviations, output the canonical value; follow the format and disambiguation notes exactly:
{% for attr, rule in extraction_rules.items() %}
- {{ attr }}:
{%- if rule['synonyms'] %}
  Canonical value <= variants that map to it:
{%- for canon, variants in rule['synonyms'].items() %}
    * {{ canon }} <= {{ variants | join(', ') }}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if rule['values'] %}
  Allowed values: {{ rule['values'] | join(', ') }}
{%- endif %}
{%- if rule['pattern'] %}
  Format: {{ rule['pattern'] }}
{%- endif %}
{%- if rule['notes'] %}
  Note: {{ rule['notes'] }}
{%- endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}
Return in 'Column: Value' format separated by '|'. For example, if the description is "{{ example_desc }}", then return
{{ example_output }}

Return exactly one such line per product, whether you were given one description or several.

Not everything will be in the description{% if cluster_descriptions | default(false) %}s{% endif %}, so you can leave things blank.
